  "worked_solution": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{X-Y}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x-y}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}[{e}^{x}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(e-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[(e-1)y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =(e-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
